"enpe7n4le02ob05aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "associate"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to assemble, to put together or to accumulate"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We might use them to keep a closer view on the way objects assume roles and associate in a given project."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob05aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "slightly "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a little, fairly or justly"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Their notation is slightly different from the one we use in sequence diagrams."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob05aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "reply "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to answer or to send a response"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Logic is essential in this point, because every message should have a reply and follow an order."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob05aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "agree "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to concur, to coincide or to correspond "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I agree. Let's get to work, then!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob05aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about communication diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a brochure explaining main characteristics of these diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Explain how a communication diagram works."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Get ready to present your brochure and ideas in class"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I search for information about communication diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I make a brochure about communication diagrams. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain how a communication diagram works. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my findings with my partners."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Continuous Tenses"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use continuous tenses in English to refer to actions in development or that are taking place in a specific period of time."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<div class=\"center_text\">  <strong>Present Continuous</strong></div> You use the <b>present continuous</b> to talk about actions happening at the moment of speaking  </br></br>  Follow this structure to make sentences in the <b>present continuous</b>:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> subject + verb to be (am/is/are) + main verb (ing form) + complement</div>  </br></br>  Example: <i>  </br></br>  <strong>I'm reviewing </strong>some notes on the features of communication diagrams. </i>  </br></br>  For negative sentences use this structure:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> subject + verb to be (am/is/are) + not + main verb (ing form) + complement</div>  </br></br>  Example: <i>  </br></br>  We <strong>are not using</strong> the notation from UML 1.x anymore. </i>  </br></br>  If you want to make question in the <b>present continuous</b>, you use this structure:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> verb to be (am/is/are) + subject + main verb (ing form) + complement + ? </div>  </br></br>  Example: <i>  </br></br> <strong>Are</strong> you <strong>using</strong> collaboration diagrams?</i>  </br></br> <div class=\"center_text\">  <strong>Past Continuous</strong></div>  </br></br>  You use this tense to talk about actions that took place in the past during a period of time.   </br></br>  Use this structure to make affirmative sentences in the past continuous:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> subject + verb to be in past (was/were) + main verb (ing form) + complement</div>  </br></br>   Example: <i>  </br></br>  We <strong>were following</strong> the diagram when the boss decided to change it. </i>  </br></br>  When making negative sentences in past continuous, use this structure:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> subject + verb to be in past (was/were) + not + main verb (ing form) + complement</div>  </br></br>  Example: <i>  </br></br>  She <strong>was not using</strong> the standard notation until I asked her to. </i>  </br></br>  When you want to make a question using past continuous, you can use this structure:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> verb to be in past (was/were) + subject + main verb (ing form) + complement +?</div>  </br></br>  Example: <i>  </br></br> <strong>Was she using</strong> UML last night? </i>  </br></br> <div class=\"center_text\">  <strong>Future Continuous</strong></div>  </br></br>  You can use this tense to talk about actions the will take place during a period of time in the future.   </br></br>  Follow this structure when making affirmative sentences:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> subject + will+ be + main verb (ing form) + complement</div>  </br></br>  Examples: <i>  </br></br>  We <strong>will be doing</strong> an update to the diagrams we use. </i>  </br></br>  Here's the structure to make negative sentences  </br></br> <div class=\"center_text\">  subject + will + not + be + main verb (ing form) + complement</div>  </br></br>  Example: <i>  </br></br>  They <strong>will not be using</strong> this diagram for the following projects. </i>  </br></br>  If you want to make questions using future continuous, you can follow this structure:   </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> will + subject + be + main verb (ing form) + complement + ?</div>  </br></br>  Example: <i>  </br></br>  Will <strong>he be using</strong> communication diagrams? </i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I am still trying to figure out what the most convenient diagram for our next project is."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I was checking some documents, and apparently we need to find a way to control the way different objects work towards a goal, right? "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Though, we will be paying more attention to the roles that each object will have; and their notation is slightly different from the one we use in sequence diagrams."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Modal Verbs - Might"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Modal verbs allow you to give different nuances or shades of sense when used with other verbs."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use the modal verb <strong>might</strong> to express future possibilities that are not very likely to happen, or that have a low probability of becoming a reality. </br></br>  In order to use the modal verb <strong>might</strong>, you need to follow this structure: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> subject + <strong>might</strong> + main verb (base form) + complement</div></br></br>  Examples: </br></br>  The communications diagram <strong>might </strong>require many adjustments. </br> You <strong>might</strong> need to use more detailed instructions. </br>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "We might use them to keep a closer view on the way objects assume roles and associate in a given project."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You might use both numbers and letters when organizing the messages that each object is going to send."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<b>Joe</b>: Denise, I am still trying to figure out what the most convenient diagram for our next project is. Can you help me? </br>\n<b>Denise</b>: I was going to ask you that! I was checking some documents, and apparently, we need to find a way to control the way different objects work towards a goal, right? </br>\n<b>Joe</b>: Yes. We need to have a bird's eye view of how they work together. I guess we can use collaboration diagrams; don't you think?"}, {"time": "28000", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob05re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<b>Denise</b>: I agree with you, Joe. However, that's the name they had in UML 1.x. Nowadays, their name is communication diagrams. As you said, we might use them to keep a closer view on the way objects assume roles and associate in a given project. </br>\n<b>Joe</b>: I see. That's the name they have in UML 2.x. Anyway, I was checking some more information about them, and it says that they are similar to sequence diagrams. Is that right?"}, {"time": "64000", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob05re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<b>Denise</b>: Pretty much, though we will be paying more attention to the roles that each object will have, and their notation is slightly different from the one we use in sequence diagrams. </br>\n<b>Joe</b>: Good. So, how will we be organizing messages in these diagrams? I was expecting to use numbers. </br>\n<b>Denise</b>: You might use both numbers and letters when organizing the messages that each object is going to send. Logic is essential at this point because every message should have a reply and follow an order. </br>\n<b>Joe</b>: That sounds perfect! I'm considering this kind of diagram. We were discussing the way we will be creating communication links between the different objects, and I think communication diagrams links might be more effective than sequences. </br>\n<b>Denise</b>: I agree. Let's get to work, then!"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n4le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le02ob05re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1,